---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import CompressImage from '../../components/tools/CompressImage';
import RelatedTools from '../../components/layout/RelatedTools';
import { relatedTools } from '../../data/related-tools';
---

<ToolLayout
  title="Compress Image Online Free — Reduce File Size | Filekrush"
  description="Compress any image to your target file size. Runs in your browser — no upload, no signup. Supports JPG, PNG, WebP."
  canonical="/compress"
  heading="Compress Image"
  subheading="Compress images to exactly the size you need — for email, government forms, social media, or web uploads. Pick your use case below, get exact specs, compress instantly. No upload, no signup."
>
  <CompressImage client:only="react" />

  <Fragment slot="content">
    <section class="prose prose-gray max-w-none">
      <div class="toc">
        <p>On this page</p>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="#decision-guide">Which size do I need?</a></li>
          <li><a href="#how-it-works">How it works</a></li>
          <li><a href="#why-it-matters">Why compression matters</a></li>
          <li><a href="#faq">FAQ</a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h2 id="decision-guide">Which compression size do I need?</h2>

      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Use case</th>
            <th>Recommended size</th>
            <th>Why</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Email attachments</td>
            <td><a href="/compress/to-200kb/">200 KB</a> – <a href="/compress/to-500kb/">500 KB</a> per photo</td>
            <td>Providers cap total attachments at 20-50MB — sending several photos adds up fast.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Government forms (Aadhaar, Passport, PAN, exams)</td>
            <td><a href="/compress/to-50kb/">50 KB</a> – <a href="/compress/to-100kb/">100 KB</a>, exact per form</td>
            <td>Oversized files are rejected automatically; undersized ones get too blurry to pass checks.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Social media (Instagram, Facebook, LinkedIn)</td>
            <td><a href="/compress/to-500kb/">500 KB</a> – <a href="/compress/to-1mb/">1 MB</a></td>
            <td>Platforms re-encode everything you upload, so extra original quality is wasted.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Website or blog (own site, Shopify, WordPress)</td>
            <td><a href="/compress/to-50kb/">50 KB</a> – <a href="/compress/to-100kb/">100-200 KB</a></td>
            <td>File size drives page load speed — smaller images mean faster pages and better SEO.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <div class="callout">
        <p><strong>Not sure which one applies to you?</strong> Government forms have the tightest, most specific limits — see the <a href="/compress/for-government/">step-by-step government forms guide</a>. Optimizing for your own website? See the <a href="/compress/for-website/">web performance guide</a> for device-specific targets and format advice.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="how-it-works">How it works</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Choose your target size</strong> — based on your use case above. Not sure? Use the table.</li>
        <li><strong>Upload your photo</strong> — drag and drop or click to upload. JPG, PNG, and WebP supported. Your file stays on your device — nothing is uploaded to a server.</li>
        <li><strong>Compress instantly</strong> — Filekrush adjusts quality and resolution together to hit your exact target size without making the photo look terrible. You'll see the before/after comparison.</li>
        <li><strong>Download and use</strong> — upload the compressed file to your form, email, or website. Done.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 id="why-it-matters">Why compression matters</h2>
      <p>File size isn't arbitrary — each use case needs a different target for a different reason.</p>

      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Use case</th>
            <th>What goes wrong without compression</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Government forms</td>
            <td>Portals reject oversized files on storage limits; undersized files fail facial recognition checks.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Email</td>
            <td>10 photos at 5MB each instead of 500KB use 45MB more of a recipient's inbox quota than needed.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Web and social</td>
            <td>Every 100ms of extra load time costs roughly 1% of visitors — slow pages lose people before they see anything.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Mobile specifically</td>
            <td>4G is around 10x slower than desktop WiFi — a 2MB photo takes ~5 seconds to load; at 200KB, under 1 second.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>

      <h3>Won't compressing ruin my photo quality?</h3>
      <p>It depends on your target size and original quality. Filekrush uses intelligent scaling: compress to 100KB and it keeps quality high while scaling down resolution slightly. Compress to 20KB (extreme) and quality drops more noticeably. For most use cases — email, web, social — the difference is invisible. Government forms tend to require higher KB targets (50-100KB), so quality stays high there too.</p>

      <h3>My photo is already 50KB but I need 20KB for a government form. Will it look terrible?</h3>
      <p>At 20KB, quality will drop noticeably — most photos do at that size. But if a government form requires 20KB, that's the limit set by their system, and Filekrush will compress to that target so the form accepts it. If the form then rejects it for quality reasons, try the next size up if it's allowed.</p>

      <h3>Should I keep the original or delete it after compressing?</h3>
      <p>Keep the original. Compression is lossy for JPG — once you compress, you can't get the quality back. If you need to re-compress to a different size later, you'll want to start from the original.</p>

      <h3>Which format compresses best — JPG, PNG, or WebP?</h3>
      <p>JPG compresses best: smallest file, fastest. PNG stays lossless (no quality loss, but larger files). WebP splits the difference — small file, good quality, but older browsers don't support it. Use JPG for email, government forms, and general web use; PNG for images that need transparency; WebP for modern web projects.</p>

      <h3>Can I compress videos?</h3>
      <p>No, Filekrush only handles images. For videos, desktop tools like HandBrake or FFmpeg work well.</p>

      <h3>How do I know if my compressed photo will pass a government form's automatic checks?</h3>
      <p>Upload it and try. If it's rejected, the error message (if the portal gives one) will usually say the file is too large, too small, or the wrong format — adjust based on that and retry.</p>
    </section>

    <RelatedTools title="Other compression sizes" tools={relatedTools.compress} />
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
